# The Crooked Moon's visits (data/ritual/visits.json, story/crooked_moon_visits.gd): the Crooked Man on the roads, at the
# village's edge and in dreams, and the second and third voices, Gorthos at camp and the Horned King in Act IV's sleep. The
# party hasn't learned his name when the first of these come, so the Narrator never gives it. The Vagrant tells it
# afterwards: second person, present tense, one or two sentences, no game words. First draft by Claude; Gemini edits it.

~ visit:cm_road_milestone
| A thin man in a bent top hat sits on a milestone up ahead, and as you come level he tips the hat like you're running late. When you look back, the stone's bare and the moon's grinning down on it.
~ visit:cm_road_scarecrow
| There's a scarecrow in the field beside the road that wasn't there a breath ago, and it's wearing a crooked top hat and a smile somebody painted on in a hurry. Its head turns to follow you until the hedge gets between you.
~ visit:cm_road_lamp
| A lamp hangs from a dead branch over the road, and under it a man in a bent hat holds up fingers as you pass: he folds one down, slow, and grins. The lamp goes out, and he goes with it.
~ visit:cm_road_glimpse
| Far down the road, a long figure in a bent hat stands where the ruts turn, and he's gone before anybody can point.
| Somebody is whistling up ahead, out of tune and pleased with himself, and the whistling stops the moment you round the bend.
| A shadow with a crooked hat crosses the road in front of you without anybody there to cast it.
~ visit:cm_edge_well
| By the well at the village's edge a man in a bent top hat is drawing water that runs out of the bucket black. He sees you seeing him, and he sets the bucket down very gently, like it's something that might wake.
~ visit:cm_edge_window
| In a dark window at the end of the lane a tall shape in a crooked hat is pressed up close to the glass, and it's counting the folk who pass with one long finger. Then the shutter swings closed on its own.
~ visit:cm_edge_glimpse
| A tall man in a bent hat leans on the last fence post of the village, watching the lights the way a farmer watches a field come ripe.
| Children's voices drift from somewhere you can't find, singing a counting rhyme, and a man's voice joins in on the last line.
| Every turnip lantern along the lane is grinning a little wider than whoever carved it meant to.
~ visit:cm_dream_counting
| You dream of a long table laid for a feast, every chair empty but one, where a man in a bent top hat is crossing names off a list. He looks up, finds yours, and licks his pencil.
~ visit:cm_dream_tree
| You dream of a tree as wide as a church with a door in its trunk, and something on the other side of that door is breathing slow and patient. A man in a crooked hat sits on the roots with a key and tells you it won't be long now.
~ visit:cm_dream_fire
| You dream of fire on a hill and a tall figure made of sticks walking out of it, and the man in the bent hat is riding on its shoulder, laughing. You wake with the smell of smoke in your hair.
~ visit:cm_dream_again
| You dream the moon leans close enough to whisper, and it uses a man's voice to do it, though you can't keep hold of a word once you wake.
| In your sleep somebody in a crooked hat sits at the foot of your blanket, patient as a creditor, and when you wake the grass there's pressed flat.
| You dream you're counting the stars and keep coming up one short, and a dry voice at your ear tells you which one it was.
~ visit:gorthos_camp
| Pipe music drifts out of the dark trees beyond the fire, merry and sly, and goat hooves dance a ring around your camp without ever stepping into the light.
| Somebody laughs out in the dark, a young man's laugh with a bleat at the end of it, and a little wreath of berries is waiting on your pack when you wake.
| A wine cup you never set out is sitting by the coals come morning, full to the brim and warm as blood.
~ visit:horned_king_whisper
| You dream of a black goat on a hill with three red eyes, and it speaks to you in your own voice, asking what you'd like most in all the world. You wake with the answer on your tongue.
| In your sleep a soft voice offers you everything you ever lost, laid out on a cloth like a peddler's wares. All you'd have to do is say yes.
| Hooves circle your rest all night long, unhurried, and the dark smells of musk and honeyed wine. Nobody sleeps much.
